## Decisions

- **Helper folder name stays `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/`.** The
  parent spec's strategy paragraph and outcomes.undesired call out
  this folder name explicitly as one of the three forbidden-token
  carve-outs. The name describes the bundled CONTENT (the default
  scaffolded brain.aide is Obsidian-flavored — Obsidian-flavored
  prose, Obsidian-flavored playbook seed bytes with `[[wikilink]]`
  placeholders, Obsidian-flavored research seed) — not a code-level
  branch. Renaming would obscure that the bundled bytes are
  Obsidian-shaped. Decision is preserved verbatim from the prior
  cycle's plan.

- **Playbook and research section bytes are STRUCTURALLY preserved
  from the prior cycle's emission.** The H1, the H3 table skeleton,
  the H3 guidance blocks, the H3 placeholder lists — all carry
  forward byte-for-byte. The user has already accepted these as the
  seed shape (the prior plan executed and the user approved the
  bytes). Re-authoring would invite drift between "what the prior
  cycle scaffolded" and "what this cycle scaffolds." The only
  change to those sections is the marker delimiters wrapping them
  — the contents between markers are byte-equal to the contents
  between the retired headings.

- **Nested `## ` → `### ` demotion stays even though the parser no
  longer cares.** The prior cycle's heading-walking parser required
  every nested `## ` inside a section body to demote to `### ` so
  the parser would not misread it as a section boundary. The new
  marker-pair walker is byte-based, so nested `## ` is no longer a
  parser concern. But the demotion stays as CONTENT HYGIENE: every
  top-level `## ` in the seeded artifact is a section boundary by
  user convention, even though the parser has stopped enforcing it.
  Reverting the demotion would re-introduce visual ambiguity in the
  rendered Markdown for users editing the file. The negative regex
  assertions in step 2c and 2d preserve this convention as a
  regression test.

- **Prose body switches to storage-agnostic vocabulary, but tool
  names and file paths are sentinels that stay.**
  `mcp__brain__read_note` and `mcp__brain__search_notes` are MCP
  tool names the agent must call by their exact registered name —
  they are not subject to the vocabulary sweep because they are
  not project prose. The entry-point file paths
  (`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`,
  `research/research.md`) are framework contracts the prose
  references for the agent's benefit; they stay as-is. Everything
  else in the prose body is storage-agnostic project prose subject
  to the sweep.

- **Playbook section's `[[wikilink]]` placeholders are NOT subject
  to the vocabulary sweep.** The vocabulary sweep applies to module
  prose (sentence-level descriptions of the brain). The
  `[[your-conventions-note]]` and
  `[[your-folder-structure-note]]` strings inside the playbook
  section are CONTENT SCAFFOLDING — placeholder anchors the user
  fills in on first edit. They live in the bytes that ship to the
  user's brain as the playbook entry-point artifact, not in the
  prose section. The user's brain is Obsidian-shaped by default
  (per the helper folder name's carve-out), and Obsidian users
  navigate via wikilinks. Removing the placeholders would orphan
  the user with an empty entry-point artifact. The
  storage-agnostic rule applies to prose, not to scaffolding the
  user is meant to replace.

- **`expect(result.prose).toContain("wikilink")` is the only test
  assertion that gets DROPPED, not rewritten.** That assertion
  asserted on the prior body's vocabulary; the new body no longer
  contains the token. Dropping the assertion is correct (it
  asserted a now-falsified content fact). The replacement
  assertions are the negative regressions in step 2j that anchor
  the storage-agnostic rewrite — they assert what the new body
  MUST NOT contain.

- **Marker placement uses single ASCII spaces around the token.**
  The parser's recognized marker form is byte-exact:
  `<!-- aide-prose-start -->` (one space after `<!--`, one space
  before `-->`, lowercase token, no extra whitespace). The
  template emits this exact form for every opener and closer. The
  parser's malformed-marker scan rejects any other whitespace
  shape (e.g. `<!-- aide-prose-start  -->` with two trailing
  spaces or `<!--aide-prose-start-->` with no spaces) as
  `unknown marker`. The template MUST match the recognized form
  exactly to avoid round-trip failures.

- **The `## Prose` / `## Playbook hub` / `## Research hub`
  headings are removed entirely, not transformed.** The prior
  cycle's heading-bounded body had three `## ` headings as
  section boundaries; the marker-pair body replaces them with
  six marker lines. The headings do not survive in any form —
  they are not transformed into H1s, they are not preserved as
  comments, they simply go away. Each section opens and closes
  with its marker pair only; the section's content starts on the
  line after the opener (with a blank line for readability).

- **Frontmatter shape is unchanged.** The schema collapse already
  settled the frontmatter to two top-level fields (`name` +
  `mcpServerConfig`); the platform branching and path-inline
  decisions all stay. This plan touches the body grammar only.

- **No new helper subfolder.** The template still emits a single
  string from a single function. The file size grows slightly to
  accommodate the marker lines but stays well under the
  progressive-disclosure threshold for splitting. If a future
  refactor introduces per-section composition logic (e.g. reading
  section bodies from sibling files), factor each responsibility
  into its own subfolder at that point.
